You wake on a narrow straw mattress, the blanket scratchy against your neck. A blade of morning light slips through the warped shutter and lands across your eyes like a hot coin.

The room is barely wider than the bed. Rough pine boards sweat tar in thethe heat. You smell hearth smoke and last night’s ale soaked into the floorboards. A clay cup waits on the stool beside you. You reach for it. The cup skitters an inch, then stops—still as if it never moved. Your pulse thumps once. You stare, but the cup only steams, innocent.

Beyond the wall, carts clatter over cobbles, a woman hawks apples, and somewhere a dropped crate thuds amid laughter. Life in Ardent Hollow is already up and trading.

The inn’s hallway c’the floorboards creak. Your boots lie by the door. What will you do first?

